onl", "quote": "On an isolated outcrop of Oolitic Limestone on the route of the Wansdyke, Stantonbury Camp was an Iron Age hill fort. Its iron sickle or pruning-hook blades now rest in the British Museum."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on an isolated outcrop of Oolitic Limestone [S3]; the site is on the route of the Wansdyke [S3]; Stantonbury Camp was an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; iron blades of sickles or pruning hooks come from the site [S4]; those blades are now in the British Museum [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-005 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stantonbury Camp was an Iron Age hill fort (in Somerset, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stantonbury_Camp]; The hillfort stands on an isolated outcrop of Oolitic Limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stantonbury_Camp]; The hillfort is on the route of the Wansdyke [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stantonbury_Camp]; Iron agricultural implements from the site include blades of sickles or pruning hooks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stantonbury_Camp]; These iron implements from the site are now in the collection of the British Museum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stantonbury_Camp]"}]'::jsonb),
